Tests for C++26 contracts (P2900) and the contracts extensions on this
branch.  g++.dg/dg.exp runs every *.C here, including the three
subdirectories, which have no .exp of their own.  *.cc and *.h files are
extra translation units and headers that a test pulls in with
dg-additional-sources or #include; *.json files are contract configurations
(P3595) named by a test's -fcontract-configuration-file option.

Naming
------

basic.contract.*, dcl.contract.*, expr.prim.*, basic.scope.*, over.call.*
	Named after the stable name and paragraph of the working draft that
	the test checks, for the base C++26 feature.

pNNNN-*
	A proposal that extends the base feature, enabled by its own
	-fcontracts-pNNNN option (see c.opt): p3097 virtual functions, p3098
	postcondition captures, p3099 diagnostic messages, p3100 implicit
	contract assertions for undefined behaviour (including the routing of
	sanitizer checks, -fsanitize-semantic=), p3290 the trigger API and
	assert, p3400 assertion-control labels, p4283 requires clauses, p4298
	the noexcept semantics, p4299 contracts for C (its C++ side), p4301
	contract_violation::report(), and p3850 the umbrella option.  p3595
	is configuration, which has no option of its own: its tests use
	-fcontract-configuration-file=.

prNNNNNN.C, prNNNNNN-*.C
	A regression test for GCC PR NNNNNN.

open-bug-*
	A watch test for an issue that is open on this branch.  The failing
	expectation is xfailed and labelled with the issue's ID (GCC-NN) from
	the branch's open-issues/ or bug-reports/ tracker, so the day the
	issue is fixed the test XPASSes and says so.  A few instead pin
	behaviour that is correct here and open in the Clang fork, whose
	mirror carries the xfail; such a file says so at the top.

matrix-*
	Generated; see "The matrix-* files" below.

callerside-checks/, outline-checks/, definition-checks/
	Caller-side checking (-fcontracts-client-check=), outlined checks
	(-fcontract-checks-outlined) and -fcontracts-definition-check=.

Everything else has a descriptive name, usually starting with what it
tests: contract-*, lambda-*, postcondition-*, constexpr-*, ce-* (constant
evaluation under a configured semantic), and so on.

Options
-------

Most tests select the dialect with { target c++26 } and add their options
with dg-additional-options, so the harness runs them in each C++26-or-later
dialect it tests, with its usual flags.  Most of the tests that set
-fsanitize= (the p3100-* sanitizer-routing tests) instead use dg-options
with an explicit -std=c++26, and run once.

A test names the contracts options it relies on: -fcontracts, or the
-fcontracts-pNNNN option of each proposal it tests (which implies
-fcontracts).  The umbrella -fcontracts-p3850 turns on every extension;
it is for the p3850-* tests and for tests that exercise the extensions
together.

A test whose result depends on the evaluation semantic pins it with
-fcontract-evaluation-semantic=, even when the value is the default
(enforce): the semantic also applies during constant evaluation, and with
ignore a static_assert-driven test passes having tested nothing, so the
explicit option guards against the default changing.  Runtime tests that
must survive a violation use observe; enforce terminates.

Every dg-do run test that can be linked with contracts enabled carries

  // { dg-skip-if "requires hosted libstdc++ for stdc++exp" { ! hostedlib } }

because the driver links -lstdc++exp and -lcontracts whenever contracts
are on -- any contracts option, or any C++26-or-later dialect -- and those
libraries do not exist in a freestanding libstdc++.

Many tests name their counterpart in the Clang fork's suite on a
"Mirror:" line (clang/test/Contracts/...); the two are kept in step.

The matrix-* files
------------------

The matrix-* tests are generated from a cross-product specification
maintained outside this tree, which writes each file and its Clang mirror
(clang/test/Contracts/matrix-*.cpp) together, so the two ask the same
question.  Each file holds the cells of one group that share a phase
(-fsyntax-only, code generation, or run), an expectation, a feature set, an
evaluation semantic and a configuration, among other axes; a file's banner
says what the group is about.  A configuration is written once per group,
feature set and caller-side setting, and shared by the files that need it
(for example matrix-readers-caller.json).  The expected diagnostics in the
error files are the compiler's own messages as observed when the files were
generated.  An edit made by hand here, rather than in the specification, is
lost the next time the files are generated.
